1 NOTE 1828 Robert was born in Mile End, Middlesex on 10/1/1828 to Thomas and Elizabeth. He was baptized at St Mary Stratford Bow, Tower Hamlets, Kent. At the time of his birth his father was a labourer.
2 CONT 
2 CONT 1841 At 13 years of age Robert is living with his parents and family in Erith, Kent. His father, Thomas, is now a Brickmaker.
2 CONT 
2 CONT 1851 In the census of 1851 Robert is a labourer lodging at Elmley Ferry Inn in Murston, Kent. The Inn keeper is Eliza Coleman, the widow of George Coleman. Two months after the census Robert married Eliza in London. The marriage certificate says th
2 CONC eir residence at the time of the marriage was East Smithfield and they were married in Christ Chuch, St George In The East.  Robert's profession is recorded as Publican.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Robert &amp; Eliza had no children together.
2 CONT 
2 CONT 1861 In the 10 years to the 1861 census there were some major changes in the lives of  Robert and Eliza. They moved to Brier Cottage in Borden and there is no mention of the Elmley Ferry Inn. Robert has become a Brickmaker "employing 43 men and 4
2 CONC 5 boys". Also present are Eliza's two youngest children, Esther and Ephriam Coleman (16 and15), and also two of Robert's brothers, Samuel (29) and Charles (19) a bricklayer and apprentice who are presumably working for the family business.
2 CONT 
2 CONT 1880 In an article by George Payne reporting on Celtic Remains discovered at the site of  Grovehurst Manor in Milton, the location of Robert's brickfield is referred to as the site where the remains were found. The article also says that the relic
2 CONC s "would probably have been lost had it not been for the courteous assistance of .........Mr Robert Watts, and of Mr Thomas Watts, the foreman of the brickyards, the latter displaying an enthusiastic zeal in the cause, rarely to be met with".
2 CONT 
2 CONT 1881 The census shows that Robert &amp; Eliza have moved to 108 East St, Sittingbourne with Robert's mother, Elizabeth, now 80 years old, living with them. Visiting is an Esther David, 
2 CONT 
2 CONT 1885 By the time of Eliza's death aged 72 in 1885, it can be seen from her death certificate that Robert has stopped working at the relatively young age of 57 and is living in more modest accomodation at 98 William Street, Milton.
2 CONT 
2 CONT 1891 The census shows that Robert has moved in with his brother Samuel Watts, now a builder, in Erith Kent. Robert's occupation is recorded as Brickmaker although this may refer to his previous occupation.
2 CONT 
2 CONT 1898 Robert died and was buried in Erith Kent on 2nd February 1898
