1 NOTE From the Laindon &amp; Dunton Parish Magazine, September 2009:
2 CONT 
2 CONT The `Peculiar People´ were a religious sect formed in 1837. One of the Peculiar People´s members, in his early days, had a close connection to Langdon Hills. His name was John Hockley; he was born in Wickford on 1st September 1861 to Samuel and Eli
2 CONC za. He joined the movement at Ramsden Heath in 1882 and was involved with opening a chapel at South Green for preaching in 1887.Thereafter he carried out missionary work for the movement in Southend, Chelmsford, Wickford and Leigh.
2 CONT 
2 CONT His father was born in Nevendon but a few months after John was born the family moved into a cottage built for them by Henry Stones, a farmer at Wickford Wick Farm. The family were very poor and at the time Samuel was only earning nine shillings an
2 CONC d ten pence a week and with a very large family it was a tremendous struggle. Back in 1837 when Samuel was only six years old he was sent out to work driving a plough and crow-scaring for two pence a day or one shilling for the week and he would ge
2 CONC t a beating if the farmer did not think he was pulling his weight.
2 CONT 
2 CONT In 1867 the family moved to Lower Dunton Hall and Samuel became the Farm Bailiff so it would appear that things were beginning to improve. John went to the village school in Dunton for the next five years. He left school at the age of eleven and st
2 CONC arted work on the farm, his first job was driving a three horse plough and occasionally he would do bird-scaring and other odd jobs on the farm, all for 3 shillings a week. On Sunday, John would go to Dunton church where he joined the choir.
2 CONT 
2 CONT In September of 1875 the family moved to a large house called `Slates´ at the top of Crown Hill, Langdon Hills near the Crown Hotel. The house today is known as `Climax´. Samuel went into business for himself opening up a general provision store a
2 CONC t the house and also started a coal merchant´s business. John did not join the business but went to work for Samuel Westwood at Goldsmiths farm, earning three shillings and six pence a week rising to five shillings a week when he was fifteen. Soo
2 CONC n afterwards John went to work at Northlands Farm, off, Dry Street, Langdon Hills and sometimes going to work on the owner´s other farm on Canvey Island.
2 CONT 
2 CONT During the building of St Mary´s, Langdon Hills new church, John and his father helped to collect and cart materials such as bricks and timber required for the building. He was in the choir on the day the church was opened by the Bishop of St Alban
2 CONC s, the Rt. Reverend Thomas Leigh Claughton. 2nd July 1877. The choir had new black gowns with white robes and they sang special hymns and Psalms. John met James Monk, the church clerk, at St Mary´s, who trained him to become a bell ringer. It was s
2 CONC everal years before the church had all its bells and in John´s memoirs he mentioned how beautiful they sounded.
2 CONT 
2 CONT By 1881 the family had moved again, this time Samuel had taken over the Red Cow a beer house in Dry Street from which they also ran a general provision store. He was also farming 30 acres of land, now helped by John. John did not get on very well w
2 CONC ith his father so in 1882 he left home and went and joined his brother Samuel at Ramsden Heath. While there he got a job at a farm in Nevendon run by Adam Offins and earned thirteen shillings a week. On a trip back to his parents he met up with hi
2 CONC s pals from the choir and learned that all those that sang in the choir on its opening were to receive half-a-crown.
2 CONT 
2 CONT After talking over the matter with his brother he decided not to take the money. By now John had been converted and joined the `Peculiar People´. His brother and sister were already members of the Ramsden Heath Chapel.
2 CONT 
2 CONT John married Sarah Gibson from Langdon Hills at St Mary´s on the 29th September 1887. His occupation at the time was that of a coal merchant. Two years later they had a son, Ernest, and were living in Great Burstead; his occupation then was that o
2 CONC f a gardener. The sect was very strict with both sexes usually wearing black with very plain laced fashions and they were expected to attend church every Sunday irrespective of the weather or their health. Services would go on all day with a shor
2 CONC t break for lunch.
2 CONT 
2 CONT The most controversial of their beliefs was that of divine healing and as a consequence they would not accept any medical treatment or allow treatment for their children. This led to many deaths especially during the period when cholera was sweepin
2 CONC g the country. This today is no longer an issue as it is left up to the individual. John stayed strong to his beliefs throughout the rest of his life, dying on 2nd October 1930 at the age of 69 at Westcliff on Sea. His last occupation being that o
2 CONC f a draper´s roundsman.
