1 NOTE c1783 From his Death Certificate it can be seen that James was born in about 1783.
2 CONT 
2 CONT 1802 On 23rd May 1802 he married Mary Grigsby in All Saints Church, Maidstone, Kent.
2 CONT 
2 CONT 1839 When his son was married in May 1839 his occupation is recorded as Publican on the marriage certificate. This was likely to have been as the owner or licensee of The Ferry House Inn at Elmley, Murston on the Isle of Sheppey.
2 CONT 
2 CONT 1841 In the 1841 census it appears that he has allowed his son, George, to take over the running of the Inn and his own profession is recorded as M.S (Male Servant).
2 CONT 
2 CONT 1843 His death certificate in August 1843 records Jame's profession as 'Waterman'. A Waterman is defined by Wikipedia as "a river worker who transfers passengers across and along city centre rivers and estuaries in the United Kingdom". Bagshaw’s Hi
2 CONC story, Gazetteer and Directory of Kent 1847 records that The river Swale is crossed by Elmley Ferry and it is therefore likely that James was the Ferryman at the time of his death.
