1 NOTE As a child Walter lived with his parents and 4 brothers &amp; 5 sisters at 1 Northend Cottages - a group of 4/6 railway cottages close to the railway line between Jumbo Bridge and Tilbury Dock Station. In order to get to the shops, school, church o
2 CONC r anywhere else it was necessary to walk up the railway line and across a field before reaching habitation. As with the rest of the family Walter attended St Mary's Church  and the  Church of England school at Little Thurrock.  Walter was the young
2 CONC est of the boys, was apprenticed and became a foreman shipwright wearing a bowler hat as a sign of his position. Satorially smart he wore trilby hats whilst out during the week and a bowler on Sundays when he went to church. Until well into adultho
2 CONC od he expected his mother to buy all of his underwear and woe betide her if she made any mistakes!  He courted Ethel, a secretary at the Grays Co-operative Society, for a number of years before marrying her. This was because of some objection by he
2 CONC r father. When they did get married it was quietly on a sunday morning and her parents did not attend.  Walter was a good gardener like his father and worked an allotment.
