1 NOTE As a child Ivy lived with her parents and 5 brothers &amp; 4 sisters at 1 Northend Cottages - a group of 4/6 railway cottages close to the railway line between Jumbo Bridge and Tilbury Dock Station. In order to get to the shops, school, church or a
2 CONC nywhere else it was necessary to walk up the railway line and across a field before reaching habitation. As with the rest of the family Ivy attended St Mary's Church  and the  Church of England school at Little Thurrock.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Ivy learned to play the piano but never as well as her sister Hilda. She commeced work in Service in London and was at one time Cook to friends of King Alfonso of Spain - Boleyn was their name. Her last position before the Second World War was as C
2 CONC ook / Housekeeper in a white town house in London where she was very comfortable.
2 CONT 
2 CONT At the outbreak of the war she came home to Grays and lived with her sister Hilda. Whilst there she drove a delivery van for Piggs, a locally well known fairly large grocer, making deliveries of food to outlying villages mostly in the South Ockendo
2 CONC n area.
2 CONT 
2 CONT After the war she became a nursery assistant at a council run nursery where she remained until she was married in her late 30's to Peter Savill a boy she was with at school.    Whilst she was there, the nursery was attended by Robert Hockley her gr
2 CONC eat nephew.
