1 NOTE Stanley worked at Thames Board Mills all of his working life. He was recgnised as a good worker and was thought quite highly of.  He married Flo Gilson who had been a parlour maid. They saved hard and were the only members of the immediate family w
2 CONC ho owned their own house.  In 1944 the blast from a 'doodle bug'  bomb damaged the back of the house. Shortly after this Stanley contracted a carbuncle and for years afterwards was never free from crops of these and boils. He was very poorly and n
2 CONC o medication seemed to help. The medical profession put the continuing ailment down to shock from the bomb blast. However, an old countryman told Lenthat he had a female carbuncle and that he would never be rid of carbuncles still it stopped breedi
2 CONC ng! Whatever the reason the  carbuncles and boils gradually became smaller in size until they just stopped appearing.
