1 NOTE As a child Dorothy lived with her parents and 5 brothers &amp; 4 sisters at 1 Northend Cottages - a group of 4/6 railway cottages close to the railway line between Jumbo Bridge and Tilbury Dock Station. In order to get to the shops, school, churc
2 CONC h or anywhere else it was necessary to walk up the railway line and across a field before reaching habitation. As with the rest of the family Dorothy attended St Mary's Church  and the  Church of England school at Little Thurrock.  Known as 'Dollie
2 CONC ', Dorothy entered domestic service as a Between Maid ('tweenie') at about the age of 13 and had a very hard young life. She moved when she was 17 to work for the Reverand &amp; Mrs TW Gibson at the Rectory in Cranham where she was much happier.  H
2 CONC er final position was as 'live-in' Cook to a family (believed to be Simpson) who lived not far from her own home at a house known as Ravenswood in Meesons Lane, Grays, Essex. It was whilst working here that she met her future husband, Ben Everett
2 CONC .  Of a very happy disposition Dorothy was well liked by eveyone who met her. It was most unfortunate that at the age of 24 she contracted scarlet fever which (so it was said) weakened her heart. She had her first heart attack in about 1929 and bec
2 CONC ame a partial invalid until she died in 1944 at the age of 46.
