1 NOTE Ben probably worked at Wouldham Cement Works before joining the army in 1916 aged 19. He was wounded in 1917 and spent some time in Cirencester Hospital. The wound, in his right elbow, turned gangrenous but, rather than amputate, the main arterie
2 CONC s of the arm were opened and blow flies allowed to lay eggs in the open wound. The resulting maggots ate away the gangrenous tissue and, with the assistance of electrical treatment, the wound gradually healed. As the arm became stronger Ben was all
2 CONC owed home and he eventually regained the use of his arm aided by regular turning of his mother's mangle - or so he said! He was never again able to comb his hair, do up his collar stud or use a dessert spoon with his right arm and he always used hi
2 CONC s left arm at work. In all other respects the arm functioned normally and Ben was always extremely grateful for the pioneering treatment he received at Cirencester.
2 CONT 
2 CONT 
2 CONT 
2 CONT He married Dorothy (Dolly) Spooner in 1920 with whom he had one daughter, Margaret (Peggy). After several casual jobs at a dry cleaners, a brewery and a farm he joined the Port of London Authority as a railway shunter. Following a temporary perio
2 CONC d and conditionally learning to swim 50 yards he was made permanent and he eventually became a foreman in Tilbury Docks - a position he would hold until retirement.
2 CONT 
2 CONT 
2 CONT 
2 CONT Ben was recognised as a kindly man even if he 'blew his top' nosily on occassions and was a loving husband, father and grandfather. His first wife, Dolly, died prematurely of a heart attack in 1944 and in 1952 he married a Mrs Emily (Dot) Tyler wh
2 CONC o was a nurse at the nursery school attended by his grandson, Robert.
