1 NOTE From a young age she and her brother, Robert, spent several years living with relatives in different parts of the country resulting in them receiving a very fragmented education and home life. Her mother having died, she left Swansea on her own (ag
2 CONC ed about 13) to join her father who was living with a Mrs Nash at Kynochtown near Stanford-Le-Hope, Essex. She lived with her father until she started work as a live-in nanny for various families. It was in this capacity whilst working at the King
2 CONC s Heads public house that she met Arthur Hockley and whom she married in 1914 at the age of 17.5 
2 CONT 
2 CONT Edith was an excellent if perhaps over protective mother who took great pleasure from her childrens achievements. A good and innovative needlewoman, she made many of her daughters clothes. She was strong minded and independant which were invaluabl
2 CONC e assets in the difficult world she found herself. As well as bringing up a large family she was forced to work owing to her husbands periods of unemployment. During the war she evacuated herself and the children to Derbyshire living in Buxton an
2 CONC d Chapel-en-le-Frith where she worked for the brake shoe manufacturer, Ferodo. 
2 CONT 
2 CONT On her return to Stanford-Le-Hope she took up a position as chamber maid with the Bata Shoe Company where she worked until she retired. She moved to sheltered accomodation in Ipswich in 1975 but after a period in which she was diagnosed as having a
2 CONC ltzheimers disease she died in St Clements Hospital, Ipswich in 1987 at the age of 90.
