1 NOTE After leaving school Arthur took a number of jobs involving horses before becoming a Delivery Boy at the Kings Head Public House in Stanford-le-Hope, Essex. It was here that he was to meet his future wife Edith Watts. His work responsibilities even
2 CONC tually included the delivery and collection of mail between Grays and Stanford in a horse and trap.
2 CONT 
2 CONT During World War One Arthur served as a Driver in the Royal Army Service Corps in France sometimes attached to the American Army and for a year in the Army of Occupation in Cologne.
2 CONT 
2 CONT After the War he entered into a partnership in a taxi business with his wife's uncle, Charles Watts. Because of a disagreement the partnership was dissolved and he became a lorry driver and Agent for the ACME Oil Company.
2 CONT 
2 CONT During the 1926 General Strike he was responsible for delivering fuel to hospitals - essential work which was exempted from strike action by National Union Officials. His work activities were not always understood by some Union members and he was t
2 CONC hreatened by some who accused him of strike breaking.
2 CONT 
2 CONT During the Second World War the firm for whom he was then working moved to Manchester and he moved with them. Following separation from his wife he lived in Eccles, Manchester with a Phyllis Mellor until, in 1970, he died aged 77, in Hope hospital
2 CONC , Salford.
